<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
                              http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.plumb</groupId>
        <artifactId>plumb</artifactId>
        <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>plumb-frontend</artifactId>
    <name>plumb-frontend</name>
    <description>
        The non-Java source frontends: Kotlin (Analysis API Standalone) and Scala 3
        (dotc PluginPhase), each lowering a source AST to the unified TAC.

        WHY ONE MODULE FOR BOTH LANGUAGES. Both frontends have the SAME isolation
        requirement, and it is the same requirement: each depends on a compiler's API
        rather than on a stable published interface -- the Kotlin Analysis API
        (experimental, "subject to incompatible changes") and dotc's PluginPhase
        (compiler-internal, only guaranteed for CrossVersion.full). A breaking
        upstream change in either must not be able to break the Java analysis path, so
        neither may live in plumb-tac.

        Because the requirement is identical, isolating them SEPARATELY buys nothing:
        the blast radius of a Kotlin upgrade and of a Scala upgrade is the same either
        way -- "the multi-language frontends", not "Java". One module expresses that
        honestly and costs one artifact instead of two.

        WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. It is still not plumb-tac. Moving the frontends
        into plumb-tac would put four compilers' APIs on one module's compile
        classpath (javaparser, sootup, kotlin-compiler, scala3-compiler) and make a
        Kotlin upgrade break the Java path's build. That is the one thing the
        isolation is for.

        Scala 2 is NOT supported: its compiler-internal API differs completely from
        Scala 3's.
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.plumb</groupId>
            <artifactId>plumb-tac</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Kotlin Analysis API. This is an EXPERIMENTAL API: JetBrains states it
          is "subject to incompatible changes" and only officially supports IDEA
          plugin development. The version is pinned in the parent POM and must be
          upgraded deliberately (re-run the frontend's own tests), never by a
          transitive bump.

          The artifact is an aggregator POM; the real code is in the
          -standalone-surface / -standalone-implementation artifacts, and the
          -implementation artifact below carries the Kotlin compiler classes
          (org.jetbrains.kotlin.cli / .fir / .resolve) the standalone session
          needs.

          WHY IT IS NOT STRIPPED. That implementation jar also bundles a whole
          Kotlin script API (kotlin/script/**) and scripting compiler
          (org.jetbrains.kotlin.scripting/**). Those look like they collide with
          the DSL script engine's 2.2.0 copies, and they do, but only on ONE
          classpath. The DSL engine runs in its own classloader that owns those
          packages (see ScriptEngineClassLoader in plumb-dsl-kotlin), so the two
          never meet. A previous attempt to strip the duplicates by repackaging
          the jar only moved the problem: maven-shade-plugin skips provided-scope
          dependencies, so the "sanitised" artifact came out as a 3.5 KB shell and
          the Analysis API lost org.jetbrains.kotlin.cli.jvm.compiler.CompatKt
          entirely. The boundary belongs in the classloader, not in the jar.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlin</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlin-analysis-api-standalone-implementation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The IntelliJ platform classes the Analysis API's compile-scope surface
          refers to in its own signatures.

          kotlin-analysis-api-intellij-api-surface-components (compile scope, pulled
          in transitively) declares methods whose signatures mention
          com.intellij.openapi.util.Pair and
          com.intellij.openapi.util.ThrowableComputable. Those two classes live in
          kotlin-analysis-api-intellij-implementation-components, which the
          aggregator declares as `runtime` scope. So javac sees a method it cannot
          type-check, and the build fails with "cannot find the class file for
          Pair".

          The dependency is therefore declared explicitly at compile scope. It is
          the same artifact and the same version the aggregator already resolves,
          only the scope changes, so nothing new is downloaded and the runtime
          classpath is unchanged.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlin</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlin-analysis-api-intellij-implementation-components</artifactId>
            <version>${kotlin.analysis.api.version}</version>
            <scope>compile</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          kotlinx-collections-immutable, at COMPILE scope.

          THE SAME DEFECT AS THE Pair ONE ABOVE, IN A DIFFERENT ARTIFACT. The
          compile-scope kotlin-analysis-api-intellij-api-surface-components has a
          field of type kotlinx.collections.immutable.PersistentSet
          (com.intellij.lang.Language.transitiveDialects). Its own POM declares
          only kotlin-stdlib at compile scope; kotlinx-collections-immutable-jvm
          is declared at RUNTIME scope by the parent aggregator. javac reads
          the classfile's field type regardless of the scope that fetched the
          classfile, so it cannot type-check the field and the build fails with

            "cannot find the class file for kotlinx.collections.immutable.PersistentSet"

          The scope is widened to compile for the same reason as Pair: the
          artifact is already resolved, only the scope changes, and the runtime
          classpath is unchanged. The version is left unpinned so it stays the
          one the aggregator resolves; pinning it here would create a second
          place to forget to update.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlinx</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlinx-collections-immutable-jvm</artifactId>
            <scope>compile</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The Scala 3 compiler. The frontend uses dotty.tools.dotc.plugins.PluginPhase,
          which is compiler-internal API: dotc only guarantees compatibility for
          CrossVersion.full. The version is pinned in the parent POM.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.scala-lang</groupId>
            <artifactId>scala3-compiler_3</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The Scala 3 standard library, declared explicitly rather than relied on
          transitively. The frontend puts these jars on the compiler's classpath so
          that dotc can resolve scala.Predef, scala.Option, scala.collection.* and
          friends; a source file that mentions any of them will not type without
          them.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.scala-lang</groupId>
            <artifactId>scala3-library_3</artifactId>
            <version>${scala3.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The Scala 2 standard library. Scala 3's library depends on it for the
          collection API's shared base types, and dotc's classpath needs it to
          resolve them.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.scala-lang</groupId>
            <artifactId>scala-library</artifactId>
            <version>${scala3.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
            <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
